                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "Participle clauses",
                    "explanation": "Las participle clauses sustituyen a oraciones de relativo o adverbiales y hacen el texto más compacto y formal:\n• -ing: sentido activo, al mismo tiempo o como motivo. Looking out of this window, I can see the street. Not being an expert, I'd like to read the contract.\n• participio pasado: sentido pasivo. Built in 1890, the building… Inspired by Mrs Green, …\n• having + participio: acción anterior. Having seen six offices, I'd say this is the best.\n\nCuidado: el sujeto de la participle clause tiene que ser el mismo que el de la oración principal. «Walking to work, the rain started» está mal, porque la lluvia no camina.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Tipo",
                        "Significado",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "-ing",
                          "activo, al mismo tiempo o motivo",
                          "Looking out of this window, I can see the street."
                        ],
                        [
                          "not + -ing",
                          "motivo negativo",
                          "Not being an expert, I'd like to read the contract."
                        ],
                        [
                          "participio pasado",
                          "pasivo",
                          "Built in 1890, the building has been renovated."
                        ],
                        [
                          "having + participio",
                          "acción anterior",
                          "Having seen six offices, I'd say this is the best."
                        ],
                        [
                          "having been + participio",
                          "anterior y pasiva",
                          "Having been empty for months, it is available now."
                        ],
                        [
                          "¡Cuidado!",
                          "mismo sujeto",
                          "✗ Walking to work, the rain started."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "Built in 1890, the building has been completely renovated.",
                      "Having seen six other offices, I'd say this is the best by far.",
                      "Not being an expert in property, I'd like to read the contract carefully.",
                      "Inspired by the first person who welcomed me…"
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "Phrasal verbs for business: set up, take on, turn down",
                    "explanation": "Al montar y dirigir una empresa salen constantemente estos phrasal verbs:\n• set up (montar, fundar): We set up the studio in June.\n• take on (contratar; asumir trabajo): We'll take on two designers. I can't take on another project.\n• turn down (rechazar): Lucía turned down Mark's offer.\n• come up with (idear, proponer): Daniel came up with the name.\n• carry out (llevar a cabo): We carried out a survey.\n• draw up (redactar un contrato o plan): Grace will draw up the contract.\n• talk someone into / out of (convencer de / para que no): Helen talked me into it.\n\nSeparables (set up, take on, turn down, carry out, draw up): con un nombre, el objeto puede ir en medio o detrás; con un pronombre, siempre en medio: They turned it down (no «turned down it»).\nInseparables o de tres partes (come up with, look into, get on with): el objeto va siempre detrás: We came up with it.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Phrasal verb",
                        "Ejemplo con nombre",
                        "Con pronombre"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "set up (separable)",
                          "We set up the company in June.",
                          "We set it up in June."
                        ],
                        [
                          "turn down (separable)",
                          "She turned down the offer.",
                          "She turned it down."
                        ],
                        [
                          "take on (separable)",
                          "We'll take on two designers.",
                          "We'll take them on."
                        ],
                        [
                          "talk into (persona en medio)",
                          "Helen talked Lucía into it.",
                          "Helen talked her into it."
                        ],
                        [
                          "come up with (inseparable)",
                          "Daniel came up with the name.",
                          "He came up with it."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "We set up Green Door Design in an office above a bookshop.",
                      "Lucía turned down Mark's first offer. She turned it down politely.",
                      "Daniel came up with the name over breakfast.",
                      "We'll need to take on two more designers next year."
                    ]
                  }
